#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096346 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "bf804d6b-ceba-558b-8cde-11d70906372f" :question/math-academy-id "e-3739" :question/problem "Consider the function $y = f(x)$ below, defined on $x\\in(-2,8).$ At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/54d2fe17d8d36a23c7c714242ffbfa12a56fd8e870b7672f1ce9b7e02f6508b4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=0,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=4,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=6$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x = 6,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the points of inflection are $x=0$ and $x=4$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096347 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "803d8981-7cc1-5f4a-bb47-21d7583e00ff" :question/math-academy-id "e-3742" :question/problem "The function $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y = f''(x)$ is shown below. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/6b146f4a7bec69f1e984b3e2aa0e3c38ea65c4464da037759b41eac2fd97325f.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative $f''(x)$ must switch sign. So, we are looking for points where the graph of $f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y=f''(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x = -4$ and $x=0.$ \n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-4$ and $x=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096348 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/id #uuid "ac3b585a-909a-5498-8c39-d8d03e378d28" :question/math-academy-id "e-3746" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection (if any) for $f(x) = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\tf(x) & = x^3 + 9x^2 + 24 x + 22\\\n \t\tf'(x) & = 3x^2 + 18x + 24 \\\n \t\tf''(x) & = 6x + 18\n}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\t\t6x+ 18 &= 0\\\n \t\t6x  &= -18\\\n \t\tx &= -3.\n}\n$$\n\nFinally,  we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3.$ So we pick two points around it, say,  $x=-4$ and $x=-2$, and compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-4) & =6(-4)+18=-6<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-2) & =6(-2)+18=6>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096349 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/id #uuid "ed7d94d2-25c5-53d9-830e-a659d304ecb1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3747" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of all points of inflection for $f(x) = x^4 - 4x^3 + 6 x^2.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation $f''(x) =0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{4}-4{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}-12{x}^{2}+12x \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}-24x+12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve $f''(x) = 0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}-24x+12 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+1 & =0 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So we pick two points around it, say, \n\n$\\qquad$ $x=0 \\quad$ and $\\quad x=2,$ \n\nand compute the sign of $f''(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}-24(0)+12=12>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =12(2)^{2}-24(2)+12=12>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d896c5c2-cb8a-50bb-a88a-1bc8c0ff8495" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293989} {:answer/id #uuid "d57e04f7-f184-5c3f-bbaa-717be4b50a1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293990} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2a7df-759d-5e41-9c72-425405692344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293991} {:answer/id #uuid "a6c4dc61-c630-59bb-a18c-49f1da03d6f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293992} {:answer/id #uuid "6a87f590-5a82-5640-8d30-ba9ddbad9b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff2a7df-759d-5e41-9c72-425405692344" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293991} :answer-field/id #uuid "93b90aab-8801-5399-896c-476180951b7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "64d9a19c-081c-568b-b24d-b0a37cb6e0bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-114280" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/4bb767047996aad50a7991915c91774e0c19d9e5c1a7c60d66dd7a6afef0fa03.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$   the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186293994 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab7e4028-fe60-53ef-bdb9-54e9a23aecdd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293996} {:answer/id #uuid "785c8a1d-26f5-59bc-9e01-c2cb2bcf8afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293997} {:answer/id #uuid "c5563378-550e-54e5-9a7c-da882add709d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293998} {:answer/id #uuid "8db7951e-5ca1-5ae7-92ad-a5fbe78b614a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293999} {:answer/id #uuid "953af0b3-e502-5714-a019-e20e99c31738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "953af0b3-e502-5714-a019-e20e99c31738" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294000} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9159a3d-f144-5d82-a41c-0c2792ba62fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186293995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "959a29f9-94e3-5ec3-bf75-8100c4fac3c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-114399" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/8f20b09e85ce71a1001e7c701ebfafa3e6ac6c8adb29e8dfd47f256f37a29f5f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x$ in $(-4,1.5)$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-1$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "781672df-c315-5e6d-85bc-4f7a19710f96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294003} {:answer/id #uuid "e6dccf90-ce56-586d-8791-9c321bebaff8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294004} {:answer/id #uuid "22ba6bf1-a7cb-5e72-a86b-0eb603e8f6d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294005} {:answer/id #uuid "35f519b3-18c2-5993-a972-769d5ecc932d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294006} {:answer/id #uuid "979bfc32-d3c9-5caa-b3c0-b9d8bb052b99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1,$ $x=0,$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35f519b3-18c2-5993-a972-769d5ecc932d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294006} :answer-field/id #uuid "173fd91a-16b8-595b-ac5a-ff6f7936ce1b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "38652526-7d23-5252-8606-9024ce5aeaf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-114405" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/8b81ef12b7193ecbaf0b9b3c12b5a7c99fd72b4562c4197700062bf654822a8c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where\n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=-1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=-1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is *not* a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at  $x=1,$  the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=-1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92a329ca-e000-513c-a624-fe32c9f29396" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093760} {:answer/id #uuid "0774774a-5064-5ba7-8b6d-ab0d82cb1f8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ and $x = 2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093761} {:answer/id #uuid "1f819151-c5cf-5b4d-8903-0cab4d515534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x =-2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093762} {:answer/id #uuid "2141b62a-7e3d-59eb-9feb-9ef7a74970db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 0$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093763} {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56b4071c-cfd6-5184-aa61-e04e7eeb99b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 2$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093764} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a4b055-23cf-5535-b288-ddb6c6e34d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186093759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c163d924-6190-52ae-9f3b-628d336cfc56" :question/math-academy-id "q-114407" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36).$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-8{x}^{2}+26x-36)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-16x+26) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}+10x-10)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-10x+10) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-2{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=2.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=3,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2})=-3{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-2(1)^{2})=-e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(3) & ={e}^{3}((3)^{3}-2(3)^{2})=9{e}^{3}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=2.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=2,$ we conclude that $x=2$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=2$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee74414b-3424-54e3-90b9-e4e79fd4e817" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294010} {:answer/id #uuid "207693c9-f7ed-5426-965e-0eaa4abfef8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294011} {:answer/id #uuid "710bf677-8835-519b-9ed4-b3d18cffa733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294012} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe4a658-2d3e-5b4e-afc9-8bcaa3273da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294013} {:answer/id #uuid "4d330736-5341-57cc-8983-c076710d7377" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294014}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe4a658-2d3e-5b4e-afc9-8bcaa3273da8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294013} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa8d8d52-727d-5d36-bb07-9153d5ee2189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ba4f29a4-295c-5fc5-b7f4-771d4a403940" :question/math-academy-id "q-114409" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+1).$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+1} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{2\\cdot ({x}^{2}+1)-2x\\cdot 2x}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{(1+{x}^{2})^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 1-{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (1-x)(1+x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=-1$  and $x=1.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=-2,$ $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$    \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =\\frac{2(1-(-2)^{2})}{(1+(-2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =\\frac{2(1-(0)^{2})}{(1+(0)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(2) & =\\frac{2(1-(2)^{2})}{(1+(2)^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{25}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ and $x=1$ are indeed points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} {:answer/id #uuid "66acfaac-58a5-5185-a6d3-c3d0d2c80a85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=-2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294018} {:answer/id #uuid "c4e389c4-06ed-5dc1-8e71-6f3093c74722" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294019} {:answer/id #uuid "09b2ac1d-4454-5f3f-98b2-c98690ed692a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294020} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf65c2b-bc08-5529-b64a-1f85068785ce" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6,\\,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "306164fa-4dd6-5c1a-a107-b6af8267d7b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-6$ and $x=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294017} :answer-field/id #uuid "98b053f5-2909-537c-919b-b0d0481d82f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "98bca65a-5215-5c3c-9419-26f83fa4001a" :question/math-academy-id "q-31219" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/5d776a61d1039639d91e2c667c68965d3e2c901cdc926e8c54e6356cf1d87c2e.png)\n\nThe function $g(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $g(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $g(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${g}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={g}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-6$ and $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44277a3b-7d90-54a7-8596-3e9b4e13f650" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294024} {:answer/id #uuid "709973d5-cb17-5751-a6e4-711cb03a9418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294025} {:answer/id #uuid "85512abc-48dd-518b-ae07-5d1c20a918ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294026} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5c393d-9ba3-52bc-986a-20dc87072efa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1.5,$ $x=1,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294027} {:answer/id #uuid "ba22f9da-d27a-5740-a1c1-7d38f04c44f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-1.5$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85512abc-48dd-518b-ae07-5d1c20a918ac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294026} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a177e67-db27-5c0f-b85d-855b60f83b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ff2afc2-c179-5c76-b9b1-32f2f6483609" :question/math-academy-id "q-31224" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/e7f211223ade0c30c2ba94b1fd4c63d2d74fd6064032d799cdbe56cc0302300c.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$ \n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3.$ \n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294029 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e60e2a7-882f-5460-b47b-f6fba84c3cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e60e2a7-882f-5460-b47b-f6fba84c3cb8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294031} :answer-field/id #uuid "a43b4729-c47f-5a47-a86d-c26a8fddf152" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1d707847-ca08-55f0-a593-95ee79b1454f" :question/math-academy-id "q-314762" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}$ defined for $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{4}}+\\frac{4}{{x}^{6}}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}\\,(2-3{x}^{2})\\,{e}^{-1/{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{x}^{6}}(2-3{x}^{2}){e}^{-1/{x}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we ignored the negative square root since $x\\in (0,\\infty ).$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=1$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{6}}\\,(2-3\\,{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}){e}^{-1/(1/2)^{2}} \\\\  & =128\\,(2-\\frac{3}{4}){e}^{-4} \\\\  & =160{e}^{-4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{2}{1}\\,(2-3)\\,{e}^{-1} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-1}<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}},$ we conclude that $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294032 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4ff57d-844e-5980-82f1-a19f34a318b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294034} {:answer/id #uuid "41299c08-50bd-5d16-b696-a7f8f54383b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294035}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf4ff57d-844e-5980-82f1-a19f34a318b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294034} :answer-field/id #uuid "55a6ba74-1094-5ea7-91fe-193e75670bc4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294033} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5378ee04-60fc-5d28-bf07-50e946d8b254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294037}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5378ee04-60fc-5d28-bf07-50e946d8b254" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294037} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb710636-c076-538d-acb6-757c880357f6" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294036}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4d32a7d-f41e-5649-9959-0b6167c0aa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-344059" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of the points of inflection for $f(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)+x$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+1 \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0$ on the interval $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check if the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}.$ So, we pick points around them, say $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16},$ $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{16}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-16\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})<0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-16\\cdot (-1)>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )=-16\\cdot (1)<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8},$ we conclude that, in ascending order, \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\,\\text{and}\\,x=\\frac{3\\pi }{8}\n$$\n\n are indeed points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294038 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "244a8ea8-991a-5546-a01e-f67f4b8c06c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294040} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f11e8f-09c4-58ef-846e-f3450b62633c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6de3588d-45bf-5afc-a746-6941c52fd3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344063" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)={x}^{2}+3x-2+\\frac{1}{x-2}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=2,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =2x+3-\\frac{1}{(x-2)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)^{3} & =-1 \\\\ x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=1.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{3}{2}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0) & =2+\\frac{2}{(0-2)^{3}}=\\frac{7}{4}>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(\\frac{3}{2}) & =2+\\frac{2}{{(\\frac{3}{2}-2)}^{3}}=-14<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=1,$ we conclude that $x=1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab06a09-0a9e-5b78-99e9-7199790a1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab06a09-0a9e-5b78-99e9-7199790a1ae7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294043} :answer-field/id #uuid "33cc9e92-05cd-5f1b-8051-2026a6ea8e03" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcd2b1f-395b-56e8-a871-9409daffb768" :question/math-academy-id "q-344064" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for $f(x)=27{x}^{2}+5x-4+\\frac{1}{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, notice that $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0,$ so this can't be a point of inflection.\n\nNow, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =54x+5-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{″}(x) & =54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}54+\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3} & =-\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3}.$ So, we pick two points around it, say, $x=-1$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{6}$, and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1) & =54+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{3}}=52>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(-\\frac{1}{6}) & =54+\\frac{2}{{(-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3}}=-378<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-\\frac{1}{3},$ we conclude that $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ is indeed a point of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956d3eda-66ca-5711-a004-a0a0a317c39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294046} {:answer/id #uuid "1147df65-da1d-5798-87e1-458a70c9229c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294047}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956d3eda-66ca-5711-a004-a0a0a317c39e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294046} :answer-field/id #uuid "837b0288-ce4d-522c-8a5a-7f7ecbe97848" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ffbdbb3-ccc3-5411-bd12-adfb9215bcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ffbdbb3-ccc3-5411-bd12-adfb9215bcd4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294049} :answer-field/id #uuid "b55743b5-67fe-5439-85c0-da800c3ece5d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "59971680-0450-5fd1-ac8d-9e7399bdc2a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-344226" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{4}+10{x}^{3}+24{x}^{2}-6x$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,\\text{and}\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =4{x}^{3}+30{x}^{2}+48x-6 \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =12{x}^{2}+60x+48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{x}^{2}+60x+48 & =0 \\\\ 12(x+4)(x+1) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-5,\\,$ $x=-2,\\,$ and $x=0,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-5) & =12(-5)^{2}+60(-5)+48=48>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-2) & =12(-2)^{2}+60(-2)+48=-24<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(0) & =12(0)^{2}+60(0)+48=48>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-4$ and $x=-1.$Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-4$ and $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are indeed  points of inflection.\n\nTherefore,  $x=-4$ and $x=-1$ are points of inflection."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294050 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d43eef2-ad3c-52f6-acbe-9b69fc17c2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d43eef2-ad3c-52f6-acbe-9b69fc17c2ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294052} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dd22af7-ab24-5343-8a8e-335f2097c620" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6cae6596-2614-5d20-9d4e-fa89cfe32b05" :question/math-academy-id "q-344242" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}+30x-42)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-18x+30) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+12x-12)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-12x+12) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x-3) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=0$ and $x=3.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ $\\,x=1,\\,$ and $\\,x=4,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2})=-4{e}^{-1}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}-3(1)^{2})=-2e<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(4) & ={e}^{4}((4)^{3}-3(4)^{2})=16{e}^{4}>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=3,$ we conclude that $x=3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=3$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0855285-378e-5c34-b35e-85aabd09a19d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294055} :answer-field/id #uuid "a7c0c38c-7dd0-5344-bcb5-cf9e5fb0e6d1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294054}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "45f9354a-20d2-5917-8cc8-23e78cc4519e" :question/math-academy-id "q-344246" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find. \n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+6x-6)+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+6) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}) \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3})+{e}^{x}(3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & ={e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}+3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So, we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $\\,x=-1,\\,$ and $\\,x=1,$ \n\nand compute the sign of ${g}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & ={e}^{-4}((-4)^{3}+3(-4)^{2})=-16{e}^{-4}<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & ={e}^{-1}((-1)^{3}+3(-1)^{2})=2{e}^{-1}>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & ={e}^{1}((1)^{3}+3(1)^{2})=4e>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ So, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change the sign at $x=0.$ So, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only  $x=-3$ is an inflection point."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097238 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "065deb0c-5d57-546a-8c8d-c914f8b17320" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095786} {:answer/id #uuid "2b03460f-477c-5b1d-b368-fe255ea85a68" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095787} {:answer/id #uuid "3f0c35f0-de90-5a67-88a5-46ccd8b6d36d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 3$ and $x = 5$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095788} {:answer/id #uuid "532d9d26-989b-5702-a201-7fe2e4b78002" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ and $x = 3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095789} {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c1cb8b-f690-5b80-b7db-a62fed7b70bc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x = 1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095790} :answer-field/id #uuid "19b35f28-2017-55f5-b4dc-61220e3e94f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186095785}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2792f399-d353-50d3-affd-f9260fbd9716" :question/math-academy-id "q-51428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/a8fadcc272d96492194b2d46f1ae66151fb4fd16b2ee42a6840ff335aa0b4262.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed: \n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=3.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\n- The point $x=5$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=5.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294056 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aee619cf-b672-535d-aa99-9e0a4f1ba22b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294058} {:answer/id #uuid "abd09782-2e50-5d87-85f8-d699f0d62659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294059} {:answer/id #uuid "c542db69-e9f2-50df-8d1d-1deada5afd0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294060} {:answer/id #uuid "fab40e02-a116-5f6e-af5f-e2c7f3f14a2c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=0$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294061} {:answer/id #uuid "91eaff08-e3df-5dae-9493-ec2b5fe2d3cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=4$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "abd09782-2e50-5d87-85f8-d699f0d62659" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294059} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4a88f32-fab3-58d5-9918-5b3ad0fec4b8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4ab27e0-8363-58c7-a12b-d356bfd8fdc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51429" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/c3b95c16aff03448b59619ba6d966a4ff645f5952468b8c2701f526185c43a64.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=0$ is not a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave down both to the left and to the right of $x=0.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=4$ is not a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=4.$ Second, the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294063 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0099e032-f2ee-573a-a359-cf60e28bc89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dda8c1e5-80b9-5b9b-99a7-625b5f52699c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294065} {:answer/id #uuid "a58fb803-a5e3-5772-b93d-159da2dbd035" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=3$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294066} {:answer/id #uuid "b9d68ec7-39ef-5a17-b10b-88a4c11523f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294067} {:answer/id #uuid "27efcb3a-6bff-5f94-8b09-a7bb01518a77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294068} {:answer/id #uuid "c65cf168-f5ee-513e-9351-d90d2226cb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294069}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9d68ec7-39ef-5a17-b10b-88a4c11523f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294067} :answer-field/id #uuid "f1c4fa01-8c61-59f1-b66b-eba7232e2152" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294064}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c0961860-b611-5cd7-8889-52b60fa3a9fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-51433" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/c9373fff819b3c54ac7aa776642019ac7d4023656aa586d70b3899f821fcfd2c.png)\n\nConsider the graph of $y=f(x)$ above. What are the points of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "The points of inflection are the points where \n\n- the function's concavity changes sign, and\n\n- the tangent line to the graph exists.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our graph. \n\n- The point $x=-2$ is *not* a point of inflection for two reasons. First, the function does not change concavity. It's concave up both to the left and to the right of $x=-2.$ Second, the function is not defined at this point, so the tangent line does not exist either.\n\n- The point $x=1$ is a point of inflection. Indeed:\n\n- the graph changes concavity from concave up to concave down at $x=1,$ and\n\n- the tangent line exists at this point.\n\n- The point $x=3$ is not a point of inflection. Although the graph changes concavity from concave down to concave up at $x=3,$  the tangent line does not exist at this point.\n\nTherefore, the point of inflection is $x=1$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2cafb6c-2891-5a88-8214-2dcd73bb90f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294072} {:answer/id #uuid "e4919e6a-3325-5735-984c-dec1b1a49d8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=1,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294073} {:answer/id #uuid "d01bd796-4926-57b3-85f3-a2a5adc6fd5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294074} {:answer/id #uuid "96a13397-5525-5281-a4cc-a5fdee6d2b02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294075} {:answer/id #uuid "3fbb92df-9c99-55af-b457-ee33d0524429" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-2$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2cafb6c-2891-5a88-8214-2dcd73bb90f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=1$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294072} :answer-field/id #uuid "51bec47b-a28b-58af-8c92-91ec6a4647e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b64247db-6208-5caa-b9cd-3324ac4191ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-79197" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/07d9d47e39d76a60fdeb7424c22d73890f37897e0d4a03da67b1b373dd2a6ace.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=1$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=1$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "977a9b6e-744a-554b-92ee-0b6343041ecf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Points of Inflection From the Graph of the Second Derivative of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dc8b157-7c31-5520-86d5-398490e4cf98" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294079} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b09eeb-837b-5d5d-8fc4-0f380227af07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294080} {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} {:answer/id #uuid "13fb1a98-3b74-5fc9-94b5-677f6f9553db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0,$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294082} {:answer/id #uuid "48c2fa81-7f35-5e0a-955d-625d74477f8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62d0cf2f-a834-5fcc-ac44-2090d7ab89f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294081} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7a6ffea-ff76-537a-8399-e287f6d1cecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1fbbf821-90cb-5820-99c9-f84523b8d2c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-79200" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14033744/assets/012c84c1dfaeb5802adbecea30c3c184a13b6802f2509e5983f6b3682fe39f79.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $f(x)$ have a point of inflection?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that since $f(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${f}^{″}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={f}^{″}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-3$ and $x=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a363f215-ed40-504a-bcae-620b7d585678" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation When Some Points Must Be Discarded"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad6b7a61-ecd1-57c1-85f4-726a7d723114" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3$ and $x=0$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294086} {:answer/id #uuid "fc0fdb3e-9929-5a8a-8a22-ffa688292a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294087} {:answer/id #uuid "a7556218-3427-500f-a7e8-2f4986b22d27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294088} {:answer/id #uuid "aa0bef79-8db6-5bbe-b576-e372add96cc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "There are no points of inflection" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294089} {:answer/id #uuid "09bea8f4-2722-5e62-9ff1-edf383b8a9f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x=-3,$ $x=0$ and $x=3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc0fdb3e-9929-5a8a-8a22-ffa688292a73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294087} :answer-field/id #uuid "d42a49c5-8573-50af-a9e2-c60c4e2bcbba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186294085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "22d8eb63-a7a6-5f30-b326-a85e616affbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-79208" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinates of all points of inflection for $g(x)={x}^{5}+5{x}^{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "For continuous, twice-differentiable functions, we can determine possible points of inflection by solving the equation ${g}^{″}(x)=0.$ However, we must be sure to check that the concavity does indeed change at whatever points we find.\n\nFirst, we calculate the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =5{x}^{4}+20{x}^{3} \\\\ {g}^{″}(x) & =20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve ${g}^{″}(x)=0,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}20{x}^{3}+60{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 20{x}^{2}(x+3) & =0 \\\\ x & =-3,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-3$ and $x=0.$ So we choose points around them, say, \n\n$\\,$ $x=-4,\\,$ $x=-1,\\,$ and $x=1,$\n\n and compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(-4) & =20(-4)^{3}+60(-4)^{2}=-320<0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(-1) & =20(-1)^{3}+60(-1)^{2}=40>0 \\\\ {g}^{″}(1) & =20(1)^{3}+60(1)^{2}=80>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- The second derivative changes sign at $x=-3.$ Therefore, since the concavity changes at $x=-3,$ we conclude that $x=-3$ is indeed a point of inflection.\n\n- The second derivative does not change sign at $x=0.$ Therefore, since the concavity does *not* change at $x=0,$ we conclude that $x=0$ is *not* a point of inflection.\n\nTherefore, only $x=-3$ is a point of inflection."}]}
